solution:
First, observe that $AC=CD$.  Therefore, triangle $ACD$ is isosceles and $\angle CAD$ is congruent to $\angle CDA$.  Also, $m\angle ACD=m\angle ACB+m\angle BCD=60^\circ+90^\circ=150^\circ$.  Since the three angles of triangle $ACD$ sum to 180 degrees, we have  \begin{align*}
m\angle CAD+m\angle CDA+150^\circ&=180^\circ \implies \\
2m\angle CAD&=30^\circ\implies \\
m\angle CAD&=\boxed{15} \text{ degrees}.
\end{align*}

Since $8=\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{-3}$ the equation can be rewritten as $\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{-3(4x-6)}=\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{x+5}$. From this equation, we see that $-3(4x-6)=x+5$. Simplifying we get, \begin{align*}
-12x+18&=x+5\\
\Rightarrow -13x+18&=5\\
\Rightarrow -13x&=-13\\
\Rightarrow x&=\boxed{1}.
\end{align*}

Since the projection of $\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}$ is $\begin{pmatrix} \frac{60}{13} \\ \frac{12}{13} \end{pmatrix},$ the vector being projected onto is a scalar multiple of $\begin{pmatrix} \frac{60}{13} \\ \frac{12}{13} \end{pmatrix}.$  Thus, we can assume that the vector being projected onto is $\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}.$

[asy]
